sketchBlocks.bodyMarkdown:
Node.js lets JavaScript run as a long-lived server process instead of only inside a browser. Express sits on top of Node's HTTP capabilities and gives the application a small routing API.

A minimal server begins with the Express package, creates one application object, and starts listening on the course's local port:

```js
const express = require("express");

const app = express();

app.listen(3000, "127.0.0.1", () => {
  console.log("API listening on http://127.0.0.1:3000");
});
```

**Line by line:** the first line loads Express. `express()` creates the application that will own routes and middleware. `app.listen(...)` starts the local HTTP server on port `3000`. The callback runs only after the server is ready. The address is localhost-only, so this course does not depend on a public network.

At this stage the server is alive, but it does not yet have a route that answers a request.

